    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 1971 Nissan Skyline 2000GT-R Is Now an 833-Piece Mattel Brick Shop Set That Can Be Customized\nRendering: Dodge Gets a Much-Needed Third Nameplate, an Affordable 3-Door Neon GLHS Hot Hatchback\n9 photos\nPhoto: jlord8 / Instagram\nThe sporty American carmaker Dodge is currently going through some pretty rough times and only has two model series up for grabs: the 2027 Charger starting at $49,995 and the 2027 Durango kicking off not far away at $43,875.\nThe family-oriented Dodge Durango crossover SUV remains popular among Mopar enthusiasts, but Stellantis won’t be able to beat around the bush for too long with it since the WD third generation has been around since 2011, no matter how many updates it receives.\nMeanwhile, the eighth-generation LB Dodge Charger is much newer, launched for the 2024 model year, but it’s also a hard sell because of the higher prices and the lack of a V8 in the lineup, while the Mustang, for example, is cheaper and has the V8 option on various models. One thing it has going for it, the Charger, is the four-door version.\nBut it’s hardly a consolation since the internal-combustion Mustang crushed the Dodge Charger during the third quarter of the year in America with 9,960 deliveries to 3,446 sales for the ICE Charger. Anyway, it’s pretty obvious that Dodge needs help, and the rumor mill talks about the brand finally coming back to NASCAR or a Charger SRT Hellcat being prepared for next year.\nHowever, a member of the imaginative guild of digital car content creators dwelling across the parallel universes of vehicular CGI has a much simpler solution: Jim, a virtual artist known as \" jlord8 \" on social media, loves messing around with all things CGI from the automotive realm, and he dreams of a reborn Dodge – the Neon.\nThe nameplate, which took its name from the chemical element, was sold under the Dodge, Plymouth, and Chrysler brands in various parts of the world, produced on two separate occasions, for the 1995 to 2005 and then for the 2017 to 2020 model years across three generations. The first two had a very distinctive feature which made the Neon easy to recognize: egg-shaped round headlights.\nNow, the pixel master wants to bring them back, imagining that Dodge never made the mistake of selling the failed Hornet in North America and instead invested into bringing to market the fourth iteration of the Neon. The CGI expert jumps immediately into action with a sporty version, revealing the Dodge Neon GLHS three-door hot hatchback.\nIt’s a simple solution – a compact hot hatchback in the same vein as the VW Golf R but with American traits all over the styling. Someone even suggested the perfect powertrain for an eventual R/T version: a four-cylinder turbo with 300 horsepower and all-wheel drive. So, what do you think – yay or nay for this potential American pocket rocket? View this post on Instagram A post shared by Jim (@jlord8)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nDodge Neon GLHS Dodge Neon Dodge Neon hot hatchback CGI revival rendering jlord8\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Dodge Durango SRT Hellcat for Sale, What’s a 2024 MY Worth After 7,000 Miles?",
